"Nano Transceiver Design for Molecular Communication Based on DNA Technology | IEEE Conference Publication | IEEE Xplore (https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/10880744/)\nciteturn127search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Publisher: IEEE ... Specifically, we use DNA origami technology to design the transceiver and DNA strands as the information molecules (IMs).\n\n# Nano Transceiver Design for Molecular Communication Based on DNA Technology\n\nPublisher: IEEE\n\n## Abstract:\n\nMolecular communication (MC) is a promising way to achieve the Internet of nano things (IoNTs), and the theoretical system has been gradually improved in recent years. Ho...Show More\n\n## Metadata\n\n## Abstract:\n\nMolecular communication (MC) is a promising way to achieve the Internet of nano things (IoNTs), and the theoretical system has been gradually improved in recent years. However, previous studies have more concentrated on system modeling, often simplifying the transceiver into an ideal point model due to the absence of a detailed transceiver design. In this paper, we choose DNA, the natural material to design the MC transceiver benefited from the promoting DNA technology. Specifically, we use DNA origami technology to design the transceiver and DNA strands as the information molecules (IMs). DNA strand displacement (DSD) reactions make up to the mechanism of the transmission and reception of IMs. Furthermore, amplifier is designed based on strand displacement amplification (SDA) mechanism, and the whole MC system works under the control of a timer mechanism which is achieved by the synergy of λ$\\lambda$ exonuclease ( λ$\\lambda$ Exo) and fuel strands, one special DNA strands. For our designed nano transceiver, we modeled the MC system mathematically and analyzed the noise based on the properties of the transceiver. Based on the simulation results, we find the nano transceiver works well in nano-scale which promises the potential for further application in the future.\n\nPublished in: 2024 IEEE International Conference on E-health Networking, Application & Services (HealthCom)\n\nDate of Conference: 18-20 November 2024\n\nDate Added to IEEE Xplore: 18 February 2025\n\nISBN Information:\n\nDOI: 10.1109/HealthCom60970.2024.10880744\n\nPublisher: IEEE\n\nConference Location: Nara, Japan\n\n# References\n\nReferences is not available for this document.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe Kinematic Principle for Designing Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid Origami Mechanisms: Challenges and Opportunities1 - The Ohio State University (https://ohiostate.elsevierpure.com/en/publications/the-kinematic-principle-for-designing-deoxyribose-nucleic-acid-or/)\nciteturn127search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 9.4 years ago; Crawled: last month; JO - Journal of Mechanical Design, Transactions of the ASME ... The Kinematic Principle for Designing Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid Origami Mechanisms: Challenges and Opportunities1.\nUR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85017192279\n\nU2 - 10.1115/1.4036216\n\nDO - 10.1115/1.4036216\n\nM3 - Article\n\nAN - SCOPUS:85017192279\n\nSN - 1050-0472\n\nVL - 139\n\nJO - Journal of Mechanical Design, Transactions of the ASME\n\nJF - Journal of Mechanical Design, Transactions of the ASME\n\nIS - 6\n\nM1 - 062301\n\nER -\n\nSu HJ, Castro CE, Marras AE, Zhou L. The Kinematic Principle for Designing Deoxyribose Nucleic Acid Origami Mechanisms: Challenges and Opportunities1. Journal of Mechanical Design, Transactions of the ASME. 2017 Jun 1;139(6):062301. doi: 10.1115/1.4036216--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUse DNA Origami as a Scaffold for Self-Assembly of Optical Metamolecules (https://opg.optica.org/abstract.cfm?uri=cleopr-2015-25E3_4)\nciteturn127search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 11.2 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Lee, \"Use DNA Origami as a Scaffold for Self-Assembly of Optical Metamo